    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's logical unit. The Cisco `ipv6 enable` command is roughly equivalent to this Juniper configuration, though Juniper requires explicit unit numbering (typically unit 0) and family declaration. I've included both interface configurations since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface's logical unit. The Cisco `ipv6 enable` command is roughly equivalent to this Juniper configuration, though Juniper requires explicit unit numbering (typically unit 0) and family declaration. I've included both interface configurations since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original Cisco config.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:101::2/96', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:101::2/96\\n##', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:101::2/96'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface unit\\n- The equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` is creating a unit 0 with family inet6\\n- Since the original config had IPv6 enabled on both interfaces (Gig1/0/0 and Gig2/0/0), both need to be configured in Juniper\\n- Juniper uses `ge` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet` for interface naming\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled by configuring the `inet6` family under the interface unit\\n- The equivalent of Cisco's `ipv6 enable` is creating a unit 0 with family inet6\\n- Since the original config had IPv6 enabled on both interfaces (Gig1/0/0 and Gig2/0/0), both need to be configured in Juniper\\n- Juniper uses `ge` prefix instead of `GigabitEthernet` for interface naming\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::2/96', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::2/96\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `ge-2/0/0` is Juniper's equivalent interface naming for GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\n- `unit 0` is required in Juniper as all interfaces must be configured with at least one logical unit\\n- `family inet6` specifies IPv6 configuration (equivalent to Cisco's `ipv6`)\\n- The address format remains the same (2001:db8:100::2/96)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::2/96'}]]": 1,
